This paper investigates the standardized existence of a ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),\quad \text{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{split} \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, particularly excluding the symmetric case when $u$ is a solution. Moreover, the issue of standardized ground state solutions is addressed in the absence of symmetry, with the use of a new approach in the Sobolev space $H^2(\mathbb{R}^4)$, with the obtained result being relevant to related equations.

